1\\) (contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\)),\n  \\(x=2\\) (contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\)),\n  \\(x=3\\) (contains \\((3,1)\\)).\n* Diagonal of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=2\\) (contains \\((1,1)\\)),\n  \\(x+y=3\\) (contains \\((1,2),(2,1)\\)),\n  \\(x+y=4\\) (contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\)).\n\nThese nine lines are the only candidates for the **single** non\u2011sunny line,\nbecause we are required to have exactly two sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Remaining points after removing a non\u2011sunny line\n\nFor each candidate line \\(L\\) we denote by \\(R = T_{3}\\setminus L\\) the set of\npoints that still have to be covered by the two sunny lines.\nThe table below lists \\(R\\) and the slopes of all segments joining two points\nof \\(R\\).\n\n| \\(L\\) (non\u2011sunny) | \\(R\\) (remaining points) | Slopes of all pairs in \\(R\\) |\n|-------------------|--------------------------|------------------------------|\n| \\(y=1\\)           | \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\)    | \\(\\infty,0,-1\\) |\n| \\(y=2\\)           | \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\) | \\(\\infty,0,-1,-2\\) (only \\(-2\\) is sunny) |\n| \\(y=3\\)           | \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | contains slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) only |\n| \\(x=1\\)           | \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\)    | \\(\\infty,0,-1\\) |\n| \\(x=2\\)           | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\) | slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1,-2\\) (only \\(-2\\) is sunny) |\n| \\(x=3\\)           | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2)\\) | contains slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) only |\n| \\(x+y=2\\)         | \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | contains slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) only |\n| \\(x+y=3\\)         | \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1,1\\) (only \\(1\\) is sunny) |\n| \\(x+y=4\\)         | \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\)    | \\(\\infty,0,-1\\) |\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Why two sunny lines cannot cover \\(R\\)\n\nA **sunny** line has slo