            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le 4\\}\n      =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must find three distinct lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}\\subseteq L_{1}\\cup L_{2}\\cup L_{3},\n\\]\n\nand exactly two of the lines are **sunny**, i.e. their slopes are different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A sunny line can meet at most two points of \\(T_{3}\\)\n\nTake two distinct points \\((a,b),(c,d)\\in T_{3}\\).  \nBecause \\(1\\le a,b,c,d\\le 3\\) and \\(a+b,c+d\\le4\\), the possible differences\n\\(c-a\\) and \\(d-b\\) belong to \\(\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\\).  \nHence the slope \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{d-b}{c-a}\\) can only be one of  \n\n\\[\n0,\\;\\infty,\\;-1,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\tfrac12,\\;-2,\\;-\\tfrac12 .\n\\]\n\nThe four forbidden slopes are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); the remaining four are\n\\(1,\\;2,\\;\\tfrac12,\\;-2,\\;-\\tfrac12\\), all of which are **sunny**.\n\nA line of sunny slope can contain three points of \\(T_{3}\\) only if three\npairwise slopes among those points are all sunny.  \nChecking the six points of \\(T_{3}\\) shows that the only sunny slope that\noccurs twice is \\(1\\), realized by the pair \\((1,1),(2,2)\\); the third point\n\\((3,3)\\) is not in \\(T_{3}\\).  All other sunny slopes occur for at most one\npair.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Any sunny line meets at most two points of }T_{3}.}\n\\]\n\nTherefore two sunny lines can cover at most four points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The non‑sunny line must cover the remaining points\n\nSince we need to cover all six points, the third line (the unique non‑sunny\nline) must contain the points that are not covered by the two sunny lines.\nThus the non‑sunny line must contain **at least two** points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)), or of\nslope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  Intersecting each of these families with \\(T_{3}\\)\ngives the following nine candidates, each listed together with the points of\n\\(T_{3}\\) that lie on it:\n\n| Type | Equation | Points of \\(T_{3}\\) on the line |\n|------|----------|---------------------------------|\n| horizontal | \\(y=1\\) | \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) |\n| horizontal | \\(y=2\\) | \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) |\n| horizontal | \\(y=3\\) | \\((1,3)\\) |\n| vertical   | \\(x=1\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) |\n| vertical   | \\(x=2\\) | \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) |\n| vertical   | \\(x=3\\) | \\((3,1)\\) |\n| slope \\(-1\\) | \\(x+y=2\\) | \\((1,1)\\) |\n| slope \\(-1\\) | \\(x+y=3\\) | \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) |\n| slope \\(-1\\) | \\(x+y=4\\) | \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) |\n\nDenote by \\(R\\) the set of points of \\(T_{3}\\) **not** lying on the chosen\nnon‑sunny line.  For each of the nine possibilities we list \\(R\\) and the\nslopes of all unordered pairs of points of \\(R\\).\n\n| Non‑sunny line \\(L\\) | \\(R = T_{3}\\setminus L\\) | Slopes of pairs in \\(R\\) |\n|----------------------|--------------------------|--------------------------|\n| \\(y=1\\) | \\(\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\) | \\(\\infty,0,-1\\) |\n| \\(y=2\\) | \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\}\\) | \\(\\infty,0,-1,-2\\) |\n| \\(y=3\\) | \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\) | only \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) |\n| \\(x=1\\) | \\(\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\) | \\(\\infty,0,-1\\) |\n| \\(x=2\\) | \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\) | \\(\\infty,0,-1,-2\\) |\n| \\(x=3\\) | \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2)\\}\\) | only \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) |\n| \\(x+y=2\\) | \\(\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\) | only \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) |\n| \\(x+y=3\\) | \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\) | slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1,1\\) |\n| \\(x+y=4\\) | \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) | \\(\\infty,0,-1\\) |\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Two sunny lines cannot finish the covering\n\n*Case A – all pairwise slopes in \\(R\\) are forbidden.*  \nIf every unordered pair of points of \\(R\\) has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or\n\\(-1\\), then **any** line that contains two points of \\(R\\) would be\nnon‑sunny.  Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(R\\).\nFor each row of the table we have \\(|R|\\ge 3\\); therefore at least three\nsunny lines would be needed to cover \\(R\\), contradicting the requirement of\nexactly two sunny lines.\n\nThe rows \\(y=1\\), \\(x=1\\), \\(x=3\\), \\(x+y=2\\), \\(x+y=4\\) and the rows\n\\(y=3\\), \\(x=3\\), \\(x+y=2\\) belong to this case, so they are impossible.\n\n*Case B – \\(R\\) contains a pair with a sunny slope.*  \nThe only rows where a sunny slope appears are \\(y=2\\), \\(x=2\\) (pair\n\\((1,3),(2,1)\\) with slope \\(-2\\)) and \\(x+y=3\\) (pair \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) with\nslope \\(1\\)).  \n\n- Suppose we use a sunny line through the pair of slope \\(-2\\) (the case\n\\(y=2\\) or \\(x=2\\)).  That line covers exactly those two points, leaving\nthree points of \\(R\\) that are pairwise collinear with a forbidden slope.\nConsequently a second sunny line can cover at most one of them, leaving at\nleast one point uncovered.\n\n- In the case \\(x+y=3\\) we may take the sunny line of slope \\(1\\) through\n\\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).  The remaining points of \\(R\\) are \\((1,3)\\) and\n\\((3,1)\\); the line through them has slope \\(-1\\) (non‑sunny), so a second\nsunny line can contain at most one of them, again leaving a point uncovered.\n\nThus in every situation the two sunny lines cannot cover all points of\n\\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The possibility that the non‑sunny line meets no point of \\(T_{3}\\)\n\nIf the non‑sunny line were chosen to avoid all six points, the two sunny\nlines would have to cover the whole set \\(T_{3}\\).  As shown in §1, a sunny\nline meets at most two points of \\(T_{3}\\); therefore two sunny lines can\ncover at most four points, leaving at least two points uncovered – a\ncontradiction.  Hence the non‑sunny line must contain at least one point of\n\\(T_{3}\\), and the exhaustive analysis above already rules out every such\npossibility.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion\n\nAll possible placements of the unique non‑sunny line have been examined,\nand in each case the remaining points cannot be covered by only two sunny\nlines.  Consequently no collection of three distinct lines with exactly two\nsunny lines can satisfy the covering condition for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
