1\\}.\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n     One checks directly that:\n     * every point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to exactly one of the sets \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_k\\);\n     * each set \\(C_i\\) is non\u2011empty and is contained in the sunny line described above;\n     * the slopes of the \\(k\\) lines are distinct and none of them is \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore all \\(k\\) lines are sunny.\n\n     Adding the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y = k+2, k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\) (which are non\u2011sunny) gives a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only forbidden value of \\(k\\) is \\(2\\); for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq 2\\) a configuration satisfying the requirements exists.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *non\u2011sunny* iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is *sunny*.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume that exactly two of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \nLet the (possibly empty) set of non\u2011sunny lines be \\(\\mathcal N\\).  \nEvery non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or a diagonal of slope \\(-1\\).  \nA sunny line can intersect a diagonal \\(a+b=s\\) in **at most one** point, because two distinct intersection points would force the line to have slope \\(-1\\).\n\nConsequently, with only two sunny lines we can cover at most two points on each diagonal.  \nThe two smallest diagonals are  \n\n\\[\nD_2=\\{(1,1)\\},\\qquad \nD_3=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth points of \\(D_3\\) have slope \\(-1\\) with respect to each other, so they cannot lie on a sunny line together.  \nThus the three points of \\(D_2\\cup D_3\\) would require three distinct sunny lines, contradicting the assumption that only two sunny lines exist.  \nHence **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non\u2011sunny, they are distinct, and every point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=s\\) for some \\(s\\) in this range, hence lies on the corresponding line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The case \\(k=1\\)\n\nReplace the diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) by the sunny line \\(y=x\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(x+y=3,\\dots ,n+1\\) together with \\(y=x\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\):\n* the point \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(y=x\\);\n* every other point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\ge 3\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=a+b\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The case \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe first cover the \u201csmall\u201d triangle  \n\n\\[\nU_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nby exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and then cover the remaining points of \\(T_n\\) by the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y = k+2,\\;k+3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (4) are non\u2011sunny and are distinct from the sunny lines constructed below.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3.1  Partition of \\(U_k\\)\n\nDefine the following \\(k\\) subsets of \\(U_k\\).\n\n* **Colour\u202f1.**  \n  \\[\n  C_1=\\{(t,t)\\mid 1\\le t\\le \\lfloor\\tfrac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\}.\n  \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n  All points of \\(C_1\\) lie on the sunny line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)).\n\n* **Colours\u202f\\(i\\) for \\(2\\le i\\le k\\).**  \n  For each such \\(i\\) set  \n  \\[\n  C_i=\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\in U_k\\mid b=(i-1)a+1\\bigr\\}.\n  \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n  The right\u2011hand side of (6) is an affine equation with slope \\(i-1\\); because \\(i-1\\ge 1\\) the line is neither horizontal, vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\). Hence the line  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\; y=(i-1)x+1\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\n  is sunny.  By construction \\(C_i\\subseteq L_i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3.2  Verification that the partition covers \\(U_k\\)\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in U_k\\).  \nIf \\(a=b\\) then \\((a,b)\\in C_1\\) by (5).  \nOtherwise \\(a\\neq b\\).  Write \\(i:=b-a+1\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\ni = b-a+1 \\le (k+1- a)-a+1 = k+2-2a \\le k .\n\\]\n\nMoreover \\(i\\ge 2\\) because \\(a\\neq b\\).  Hence \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \nRearranging \\(b=(i-1)a+1\\) shows that \\((a,b)\\in C_i\\).  \nThus every point of \\(U_k\\) belongs to exactly one of the sets \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3.3  Distinctness of the sunny lines\n\nThe slopes of the lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{slope}(L_1)=1,\\qquad \n\\operatorname{slope}(L_i)=i-1\\;(2\\le i\\le k).\n\\]\n\nThese numbers are pairwise different, so the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct.  \nNone of the slopes equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently all \\(k\\) lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3.4  Completion of the construction\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\n\\bigl\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{x+y=s\\mid s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(U_k\\) lies on one of the sunny lines \\(L_i\\) by the partition proved above.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\setminus U_k\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\) and therefore lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\), which belongs to the second part of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nHence **all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k=2\\) (Section\u202f1) with the explicit constructions for all other admissible values (Section\u202f2) we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nIn words: for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only forbidden number of sunny lines is \\(k=2\\); every other integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized."

