herefore lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\), which belongs to the second part of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \n> Hence **all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered**.\u201d\n\n- **Verification:**  \n  - The set \\(\\{x+y=s\\mid s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) consists of \\(n-k\\) distinct non\u2011sunny diagonal lines.  \n  - By construction, each point with sum \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\) lies on exactly one of those diagonals, so it is covered.  \n  - The earlier partition guarantees that every point with sum \\(\\le k+1\\) lies on one of the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n  - All lines are distinct (the sunny lines have distinct intercepts; the diagonals have distinct sums).  \n  Hence the construction for any \\(k\\ge3\\) is correct.\n\n### 6. Final answer\n\n> \u201c\\[\n> \\boxed{\n> \\begin{cases}\n> k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4,\\\\[2mm]\n> k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3 .\n> \\end{cases}}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n- **Verification:**  \n  - Because the impossibility claim for \\(k=2\\) is false (see Section\u202f2), the set of admissible \\(k\\) must **include** \\(k=2\\) for every \\(n\\ge4\\).  \n  - Therefore the stated answer omits a valid value and is **incomplete**. This follows directly from the critical error identified in the impossibility proof.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:** The solution contains two critical errors (the false impossibility proof for \\(k=2\\) and the incorrect claim about pairwise\u2011different slopes). The first error invalidates the main conclusion about the set of admissible \\(k\\); consequently the final answer is wrong. The solution is therefore **invalid**."
