agonal \\(D_s\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal D\\) by definition (1), and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(D_s\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\n*Case 2: \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\).*  \nThe diagonal \\(D_s\\) consists of the points  \n\n\\[\nD_s\\cap T_n=\\{(1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1)\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|D_s\\cap T_n|=s-1\\le k\\).\n\nFor a fixed \\(s\\) and any \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) the line \\(L_i\\) meets the diagonal \\(D_s\\) in exactly one point, because two distinct non\u2011parallel lines intersect in a single point.  Moreover, if \\(i\\neq j\\) then the slopes of \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_j\\) differ, so the intersection points \\(L_i\\cap D_s\\) and \\(L_j\\cap D_s\\) are distinct.  Consequently the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) provide \\(k\\) distinct points on \\(D_s\\).\n\nSince \\(k\\ge s-1\\), the set (5) is a subset of those \\(k\\) intersection points; therefore every point of \\(D_s\\cap T_n\\) lies on some \\(L_i\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\n*Case 3: \\(s=n+1\\) (the longest diagonal).*  \nEach point \\(P_i\\) defined in (2) lies on both \\(D_{n+1}\\) and the sunny line \\(L_i\\); thus all points of \\(D_{n+1}\\) are covered.  (When \\(k=n\\) the set \\(\\mathcal D\\) is empty, and the argument above with the \\(n\\) lines \\(L_i\\) still works because for any diagonal \\(D_s\\) the \\(n\\) distinct intersection points supplied by the \\(L_i\\) already contain all \\(s-1\\le n\\) points of \\(D_s\\).)\n\nSince the three cases exhaust all possibilities for \\(s\\), every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The extreme case \\(k=n\\)\n\nWhen \\(k=n\\) the set \\(\\mathcal D\\) is empty, and we use the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) defined by (3).  \nFor any diagonal \\(D_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)) the \\(n\\) lines intersect it in \\(n\\) distinct points, while \\(|D_s\\cap T_n|=s-1\\le n\\); therefore all points of every diagonal are among those intersections, and the whole set \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a concrete family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Hence **all** such \\(k\\) are attainable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For each }n\\ge3\\text{, the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]"

