            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *non‑sunny* iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is *sunny*.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\;(0\\le k\\le n)\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\).  \nDefine the set of “kept” diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D:=\\{\\,D_s:x+y=s\\mid s=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-k\\) lines in \\(\\mathcal D\\); each of them is non‑sunny.\n\nChoose the \\(k\\) points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(P_i\\) belong to the longest diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) and are distinct because their first coordinates are distinct.\n\nFor each \\(i\\) let \\(L_i\\) be the line through the origin \\((0,0)\\) and \\(P_i\\).  \nSince \\(P_i\\neq(0,0)\\), the equation of \\(L_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=\\frac{n+1-i}{i}\\,x\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{n+1-i}{i}\\) is a positive rational number; it is never \\(0\\), never undefined (vertical), and never \\(-1\\).  Hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nBecause the slopes \\(\\dfrac{n+1-i}{i}\\) are distinct for distinct \\(i\\), the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nThe family of lines we use is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F:=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\mathcal D .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIt contains exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Verification that \\(\\mathcal F\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\)\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Set \\(s:=a+b\\) (so \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).\n\n*Case 1: \\(s\\ge k+2\\).*  \nThen the diagonal \\(D_s\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal D\\) by definition (1), and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(D_s\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\n*Case 2: \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\).*  \nThe diagonal \\(D_s\\) consists of the points  \n\n\\[\nD_s\\cap T_n=\\{(1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1)\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|D_s\\cap T_n|=s-1\\le k\\).\n\nFor a fixed \\(s\\) and any \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) the line \\(L_i\\) meets the diagonal \\(D_s\\) in exactly one point, because two distinct non‑parallel lines intersect in a single point.  Moreover, if \\(i\\neq j\\) then the slopes of \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_j\\) differ, so the intersection points \\(L_i\\cap D_s\\) and \\(L_j\\cap D_s\\) are distinct.  Consequently the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) provide \\(k\\) distinct points on \\(D_s\\).\n\nSince \\(k\\ge s-1\\), the set (5) is a subset of those \\(k\\) intersection points; therefore every point of \\(D_s\\cap T_n\\) lies on some \\(L_i\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\n*Case 3: \\(s=n+1\\) (the longest diagonal).*  \nEach point \\(P_i\\) defined in (2) lies on both \\(D_{n+1}\\) and the sunny line \\(L_i\\); thus all points of \\(D_{n+1}\\) are covered.  (When \\(k=n\\) the set \\(\\mathcal D\\) is empty, and the argument above with the \\(n\\) lines \\(L_i\\) still works because for any diagonal \\(D_s\\) the \\(n\\) distinct intersection points supplied by the \\(L_i\\) already contain all \\(s-1\\le n\\) points of \\(D_s\\).)\n\nSince the three cases exhaust all possibilities for \\(s\\), every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The extreme case \\(k=n\\)\n\nWhen \\(k=n\\) the set \\(\\mathcal D\\) is empty, and we use the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) defined by (3).  \nFor any diagonal \\(D_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)) the \\(n\\) lines intersect it in \\(n\\) distinct points, while \\(|D_s\\cap T_n|=s-1\\le n\\); therefore all points of every diagonal are among those intersections, and the whole set \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a concrete family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Hence **all** such \\(k\\) are attainable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For each }n\\ge3\\text{, the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
