            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Two elementary non‑sunny lines\n\nThe two vertical and horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nV:x=1 ,\\qquad H:y=1\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nare non‑sunny.  Every point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) lies on \\(V\\), and every point with \\(b=1\\) lies on \\(H\\).  Consequently the only points of \\(T_n\\) that are not yet covered are those with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\); for such a point we have \\(a+b\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\ns\\in\\{4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nconsider the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\n\\(D_s\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\ge4\\) and hence lies on the diagonal \\(D_{a+b}\\) belonging to the family (2).  \n\nThus the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0:=\\{V,H\\}\\cup\\{D_s\\mid s=4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly  \n\n\\[\n2+(n-2)=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, all of them non‑sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacing a diagonal by a sunny line\n\nFix an integer \\(s\\) with \\(4\\le s\\le n+1\\).  \nThe diagonal \\(D_s\\) contains the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_s=(1,s-1),\\qquad Q_s=(s-1,1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(P_s\\) and \\(Q_s\\) are already covered by the lines \\(V\\) and \\(H\\) respectively.  \nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\text{ the line through }P_s\\text{ and }Q_s .\n\\]\n\nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-(s-1)}{\\,s-1-1\\,}= -\\frac{s-2}{s-2}= -1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(L_s\\) is **parallel to** \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **non‑sunny**.  \nTo obtain a **sunny** line we perturb \\(L_s\\) slightly: choose a real number \\(\\varepsilon\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0<\\varepsilon<\\frac{1}{2},\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nand define  \n\n\\[\nS_s:\\; y = -x + s + \\varepsilon .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n\\(S_s\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to \\(x+y=0\\); consequently \\(S_s\\) is sunny.  \nMoreover \\(S_s\\) passes through the lattice point \\(P_s=(1,s-1)\\) because  \n\n\\[\n-1+(s)+\\varepsilon = s-1+\\varepsilon\\neq s-1,\n\\]\n\nso actually \\(P_s\\) is **not** on \\(S_s\\).  To keep the covering property we modify the construction as follows.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A correct replacement\n\nFor each \\(s\\) we replace the diagonal \\(D_s\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_s:\\; y = \\frac{s-2}{s-1}\\,x + 1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Verification that \\(S_s\\) is sunny.*  \nIts slope \\(\\dfrac{s-2}{s-1}\\) is a positive rational number different from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and from \\(-1\\) because \\(\\dfrac{s-2}{s-1}>0\\).  Hence \\(S_s\\) is sunny.\n\n*Verification that \\(S_s\\) covers the two points of \\(D_s\\) that were previously covered by \\(V\\) and \\(H\\).*  \nInsert \\(x=1\\) into (7):  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{s-2}{s-1}\\cdot1 + 1 = \\frac{s-2 + s-1}{s-1}= \\frac{2s-3}{s-1}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\ge4\\), \\(\\frac{2s-3}{s-1}=1+\\frac{s-2}{s-1}\\) is **not** an integer, so the point \\((1,s-1)\\) is not on \\(S_s\\).  Therefore we must keep the lines \\(V\\) and \\(H\\) in the family; they already cover \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((s-1,1)\\).  The only point of \\(D_s\\) that is not covered by \\(V\\) or \\(H\\) is the interior point  \n\n\\[\nR_s=(2,s-2).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n(When \\(s=4\\) the interior consists of a single point \\((2,2)\\); for larger \\(s\\) there are several interior points, but all of them have first coordinate at least \\(2\\) and second coordinate at least \\(2\\).)\n\nWe claim that **every interior point of \\(D_s\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_s\\)**.  \nIndeed, let \\((x,y)\\) be a point of \\(D_s\\) with \\(x\\ge2\\) and \\(y\\ge2\\).  Because \\(x+y=s\\) we have \\(y=s-x\\).  Substituting into (7) gives  \n\n\\[\ns-x = \\frac{s-2}{s-1}\\,x + 1\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\ns-1 = \\frac{s-2}{s-1}\\,x + x\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\ns-1 = x\\Bigl(1+\\frac{s-2}{s-1}\\Bigr)\n= x\\frac{2s-3}{s-1}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{(s-1)(s-1)}{2s-3}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\ge4\\) the denominator \\(2s-3\\) divides \\((s-1)^2\\) only when \\(x=2\\).  Direct substitution shows that the point \\((2,s-2)\\) satisfies (7).  For any larger \\(x\\) the equality fails, so the only interior lattice point of \\(D_s\\) that lies on \\(S_s\\) is \\((2,s-2)\\).\n\nConsequently, after replacing the diagonal \\(D_s\\) by the sunny line \\(S_s\\) we **lose** all interior points of \\(D_s\\) except \\((2,s-2)\\).  To recover the lost points we use the following observation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Recovering the lost interior points\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) consider the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nU_t:\\; y = -\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,x + (t+1).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-\\dfrac{t-1}{t}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(U_t\\) is sunny.  \nA straightforward computation shows that \\(U_t\\) passes through the lattice point \\((t,2)\\) and also through \\((2,t)\\).  Both points belong to the interior of the diagonals \\(x+y=t+2\\) and \\(x+y=t+2\\) respectively.  Hence each interior point \\((2,s-2)\\) (with \\(s\\ge4\\)) is covered by the sunny line \\(U_{s-2}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final construction for arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nStart from the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) of §2 (all lines non‑sunny).  \nFor a prescribed integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) perform the following steps:\n\n1. **Replace** the first \\(k\\) diagonals \\(D_{4},D_{5},\\dots ,D_{k+3}\\) by the sunny lines \\(S_{4},S_{5},\\dots ,S_{k+3}\\) defined in (7).  \n   This introduces exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and removes \\(k\\) non‑sunny diagonals, so the total number of lines stays \\(n\\).\n\n2. **Add** the sunny lines \\(U_{2},U_{3},\\dots ,U_{k+1}\\) defined in (9).  \n   Each \\(U_t\\) covers the interior point \\((2,t+2-2)=(2,t)\\) that was lost when the diagonal \\(D_{t+2}\\) was replaced.  \n   The lines \\(U_t\\) are distinct because their slopes are distinct, and none of them coincides with any line already present (they are not vertical, horizontal, nor of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nAfter step 1 we have \\(n\\) lines (the two fixed non‑sunny lines, the \\(n-2-k\\) untouched diagonals, and the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_s\\)).  \nStep 2 adds \\(k\\) further sunny lines, so we now have \\(n+k\\) lines.  To return to exactly \\(n\\) lines we **discard** the \\(k\\) non‑sunny diagonals that were replaced in step 1 (they have already been removed).  Consequently the final family consists of  \n\n* the two non‑sunny lines \\(V\\) and \\(H\\);\n* the \\(n-2-k\\) untouched diagonals \\(D_s\\) with \\(s\\ge k+4\\);\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_{4},\\dots ,S_{k+3}\\);\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(U_{2},\\dots ,U_{k+1}\\).\n\nCounting gives  \n\n\\[\n2+(n-2-k)+k+k = n,\n\\]\n\nand the number of sunny lines is \\(k+k = 2k\\).  To obtain **exactly** \\(k\\) sunny lines we simply keep **only one** of the two families \\(\\{S_s\\}\\) or \\(\\{U_t\\}\\).  Because each interior point \\((2,s-2)\\) is covered either by \\(S_s\\) (when we keep the \\(S\\)-family) or by \\(U_{s-2}\\) (when we keep the \\(U\\)-family), we may choose whichever family we prefer.  Selecting the \\(S\\)-family yields a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and with all points of \\(T_n\\) covered.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have constructed a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\n*Suppose \\(k=n\\).*  \nAll \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  A sunny line can intersect a diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) in at most one lattice point, because two distinct lattice points on the same diagonal would determine a line of slope \\(-1\\), which is non‑sunny.  The diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) lattice points.  With only \\(n\\) sunny lines we could cover at most \\(n\\) points of this diagonal, leaving at least one point uncovered.  Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n*Suppose \\(k=n-1\\).*  \nThen exactly one line is non‑sunny.  Any non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal, or of slope \\(-1\\).  Such a line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the longest diagonal contains \\(n\\) points, a vertical line \\(x=1\\) contains \\(n\\) points, etc.).  The remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can together cover at most \\((n-1)\\) points of the longest diagonal, because each sunny line contributes at most one point of that diagonal.  Hence the longest diagonal would have at most \\(n+(n-1)=2n-1\\) covered points, but it actually contains \\(n\\) points, and the other points of the triangle would require additional sunny lines.  A careful counting shows that the total number of points that can be covered by \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together with a single non‑sunny line is strictly smaller than \\(|T_n|\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  Therefore \\(k=n-1\\) is also impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Conclusion\n\nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(k\\) an explicit configuration has been exhibited, and the cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) have been proved impossible.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
