e fact that a sunny line can intersect a diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) in at most one lattice point, whereas the diagonal \\(x+y= n+1\\) contains \\(n\\) lattice points; with only \\(n\\) sunny lines we could cover at most \\(n\\) points of that diagonal, leaving at least one uncovered.  The case \\(k=n-1\\) is ruled out by a counting argument: with only one non‑sunny line we can cover at most \\(n\\) points of the longest diagonal, but that diagonal contains \\(n\\) points, and the remaining points of the triangle would require more than one sunny line.\n\nHence the admissible values are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Two elementary non‑sunny lines\n\nThe two vertical and horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nV:x=1 ,\\qquad H:y=1\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nare non‑sunny.  Every point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) lies on \\(V\\), and every point with \\(b=1\\) lies on \\(H\\).  Consequently the only points of \\(T_n\\) that are not yet covered are those with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\); for such a point we have \\(a+b\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\ns\\in\\{4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nconsider the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\n\\(D_s\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\ge4\\) and hence lies on the diagonal \\(D_{a+b}\\) belonging to the family (2).  \n\nThus the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0:=\\{V,H\\}\\cup\\{D_s\\mid s=4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly  \n\n\\[\n2+(n-2)=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, all of them non‑sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacing a diagonal by a sunny line\n\nFix an integer \\(s\\) with \\(4\\le s\\le n+1\\).  \nThe diagonal \\(D_s\\) contains the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_s=(1,s-1),\\qquad Q_s=(s-1,1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(P_s\\) and \\(Q_s\\) are already covered by the lines \\(V\\) and \\(H\\) respectively.  \nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\text{ the line through }P_s\\text{ and }Q_s .\n\\]\n\nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-(s-1)}{\\,s-1-1\\,}= -\\frac{s-2}{s-2}= -1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(L_s\\) is **parallel to** \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **non‑sunny**.  \nTo obtain a **sunny** line we perturb \\(L_s\\) slightly: choose a real number \\(\\varepsilon\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0<\\varepsilon<\\frac{1}{2},\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nand define  \n\n\\[\nS_s:\\; y = -x + s + \\varepsilon .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n\\(S_s\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to \\(x+y=0\\); consequently \\(S_s\\) is sunny.  \nMoreover \\(S_s\\) passes through the lattice point \\(P_s=(1,s-1)\\) because  \n\n\\[\n-1+(s)+\\varepsilon = s-1+\\varepsilon\\neq s-1,\n\\]\n\nso actually \\(P_s\\) is **not** on \\(S_s\\).  To keep the covering property we modify the construction as follows.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A correct replacement\n\nFor each \\(s\\) we replace the diagonal \\(D_s\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_s:\\; y = \\frac{s-2}{s-1}\\,x + 1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Verification that \\(S_s\\) is sunny.*  \nIts slope \\(\\dfrac{s-2}{s-1}\\) is a positive rational number different from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and from \\(-1\\) because \\(\\dfrac{s-2}{s-1}>0\\).  Hence \\(S_s\\) is sunny.\n\n*Verification that \\(S_s\\) covers the two points of \\(D_s\\) that were previously covered by \