 either vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=s\\;(s=4,5,\\dots ,n+1)\\;}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe first two are vertical and horizontal, the remaining \\(n-2\\) are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); all are non\u2011sunny.\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies one of the following:\n\n* if \\(a=1\\) it lies on \\(x=1\\);\n* if \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(y=1\\);\n* otherwise \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\ge4\\); then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=a+b\\), which is one of the lines in (1).\n\nHence the family (1) covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{is always attainable.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration with exactly one sunny line (\\(k=1\\))\n\nKeep the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \nUse all diagonals \\(x+y=s\\) for \\(s=5,6,\\dots ,n+1\\) (i.e. omit the diagonal \\(x+y=4\\)).  \nReplace the omitted diagonal by the line  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;L:\\;y=2x-2\\;}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L\\) passes through the unique interior point of the omitted diagonal,\n\\((2,2)\\), because \\(2=2\\cdot2-2\\).  \nIts slope is \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(L\\) is sunny.\n\nThe set of lines\n\n\\[\n\\{x=1,\\;y=1\\}\\cup\\{x+y=s\\mid s=5,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\cup\\{L\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(2+(n-2-1)+1=n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and\ncovers every point of \\(T_n\\) (the points on the omitted diagonal are covered by \\(L\\), all other points are covered as in the construction of \u00a71).  Hence\n\n\\[\nk=1\\quad\\text{is attainable for every }n\\ge3 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bound: impossibility of \\(k\\ge2\\)\n\nDefine the *interior* points  \n\n\\[\nI_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(4\\le s\\le n+1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in I_n\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|D_s|=s-3\\) (the points \\((2,s-2),\\,(3,s-3),\\dots ,(s-2,2)\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most one point of any fixed diagonal \\(D_s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contained two distinct points of \\(D_s\\), those two points would have\ndifference vector \\((\\Delta x,\\Delta y)\\) with \\(\\Delta y=-\\Delta x\\); the slope would be \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nIf the diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) is **not** among the \\(n\\) chosen lines, then at least \\(|D_s|=s-3\\) distinct sunny lines are required to cover the points of \\(D_s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(D_s\\); there are \\(|D_s|\\) points to be covered, so at least that many sunny lines are necessary. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nNow suppose a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists (\\(k\\ge0\\)).\nBecause the points on the sides \\(a=1\\) and \\(b=1\\) must be covered, we may assume that the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) are present (otherwise some side point would be uncovered).  \nConsequently the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are either diagonals \\(x+y=s\\) or sunny lines.\n\nHence at most \\(k\\) of the diagonals \\(x+y=s\\) (with \\(4\\le s\\le n+1\\)) can be omitted; denote the omitted diagonals by\n\\(x+y=s_1,\\dots ,x+y=s_k\\) with \\(4\\le s_1<\\dots <s_k\\le n+1\\).\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 the omitted diagonals force at least  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(s_i-3)\\ge\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(4+i-3)=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}i=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.  Since we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, the inequality  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\]\n\nmust hold.  This inequality is satisfied only for \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\) (for \\(k\\ge2\\) the right\u2011hand side exceeds the left\u2011hand side).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\ge2\\text{ is impossible for any }n\\ge3.\\;}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nFrom (2), (4) and (5) we obtain that the only non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\nBoth values are realized by the explicit constructions given in \u00a7\u00a7\u202f1 and\u202f2, and no larger \\(k\\) can occur. \u220e"
